        "Carlton M. Caves",
        "H. Jeff Kimble",
        "Nergis Mavalvala"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Squeezed light made quantum noise a controllable resource and enabled precision interferometry beyond the standard quantum limit, including in gravitational-wave detection.",
        "attribution": "Caves established the theoretical framework, Kimble helped demonstrate and develop squeezed-light methods, and Mavalvala helped bring them into gravitational-wave observatories.",
        "maturity": "The technique has been demonstrated in increasingly capable detectors and has delivered measurable sensitivity gains.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The contribution is spread across theory, optics experiments, and large observatory teams, so the division of credit is consequential."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B039",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "David Huang",
        "Eric A. Swanson",
        "James G. Fujimoto"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical coherence tomography created a new way to image tissue microstructure in depth and became a major diagnostic tool, with broad impact beyond physics.",
        "attribution": "The credited names are closely associated with the original development and demonstration; the discovery's clinical reach also reflects substantial later work by others.",
        "maturity": "The method has had decades of validation and widespread clinical use.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Its strong medical impact raises a question about how the prize should weigh a physics invention against its clinical applications."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B007",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "David E. Pritchard",
        "Mark A. Kasevich"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Atom interferometry opened a precise way to measure acceleration, rotation, and gravity with matter waves, linking quantum coherence to practical sensing.",
        "attribution": "Pritchard's atom-optics contributions and Kasevich's interferometry work are central; the broader field also depended on important contributions from others.",
        "maturity": "The method is well established in laboratory measurements, while robust field instruments and their eventual reach remain under development.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The boundary between foundational demonstrations and later instrument development complicates attribution."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
